\description{
The Bayesian flagship of cpaic. The relative effect of every treatment is
the sum of its component effects, estimated jointly from individual patient
data (IPD) and aggregate data (AgD). Aggregate arms are fitted by
integrating the individual-level model over each study's covariate
distribution, averaging the outcome on its natural scale (not the link
scale). Because disconnected sub-networks share component parameters, the
network is connected by construction.
}
\details{
The model includes component x effect-modifier interactions \code{gamma}, so the
average conditional link-scale treatment effect depends on target means:
\deqn{\theta_t(x) = C_t' (\beta + \Gamma x).}
The component main effects \code{beta} are the effects at the covariate origin
(\code{x = 0}) and are not marginally standardized quantities. Use \code{newdata} in
\code{\link[=relative_effects]{relative_effects()}} / \code{\link[=component_effects]{component_effects()}} to obtain average conditional
link-scale effects at named target effect-modifier means. Use
\code{marginal_effects()} with an explicit target covariate distribution to
standardize treatment-specific outcomes before forming marginal contrasts.

Supported families: \code{"binomial"} (logit), \code{"gaussian"} (identity),
\code{"poisson"} (log), and \code{"survival"}.
}
\section{Integration}{

Aggregate covariates are integrated with Sobol' quasi-Monte-Carlo points
coupled by a Gaussian copula, whose correlation is pooled \emph{within} IPD
studies on the Fisher z scale (or supplied via \code{cor}). Each covariate is
pushed through its own marginal inverse CDF: \code{margins} may be \code{"normal"}
(using \code{x_mean} and \code{x_sd}) or \code{"bernoulli"} (using \code{x_mean} as the
prevalence). Margins default to Bernoulli for covariates that are 0/1 in the
IPD and normal otherwise; a normal margin on a binary covariate would
integrate over a population that cannot occur.
}

\section{Random effects}{

\code{trt_effects = "random"} adds study-arm deviations around the
component-implied relative effects. Deviations use a non-centered
parameterization by default. Within a multi-arm study, deviations relative
to the study baseline have the standard NMA correlation of 0.5. The
heterogeneity standard deviation \code{tau} has a half-normal(0, 1) prior by
default. The centered parameterization is available only to reproduce
sampling comparisons.
}

\section{Priors}{

Defaults follow the Stan prior-choice recommendations. Component effects
use normal(0, 2.5), component by effect-modifier interactions use
normal(0, 1), study intercepts use normal(0, 2.5), and \code{tau} uses
half-normal(0, 1). Interaction priors do real regularization when Gamma is
weakly identified, so every fitted object records the complete prior
specification. Use \code{\link[=prior_sensitivity]{prior_sensitivity()}} to quantify contrast movement and
\code{prior_predictive = TRUE} with \code{\link[=prior_predictive_check]{prior_predictive_check()}} to inspect prior
implications before fitting the likelihood.
}

\section{Survival}{

Survival uses the exact individual likelihood ported from \code{multinma}
(Phillippo et al. 2020). The model evaluates a hazard basis and its
integrated cumulative-hazard basis at every outcome, interval start, and
delayed-entry time. It supports observed events, right censoring, left
censoring, interval censoring, and delayed entry. \code{baseline = "piecewise"}
gives a piecewise-exponential baseline; \code{baseline = "mspline"} gives a
continuous cubic M-spline baseline.

Aggregate survival input must contain reconstructed event and censoring rows
with the same outcome-time columns as IPD, plus repeated arm-level covariate
summaries. The likelihood of every aggregate row is averaged over its
covariate integration points with \code{log_sum_exp}. Aggregate event counts and
person-time alone cannot recover this likelihood and are rejected explicitly.

Two qualifications, so that "exact" is not read more broadly than it should
be.
\itemize{
\item \strong{The likelihood is exact; the covariate integration is not.} Every
individual contribution (event, right, left and interval censoring, delayed
entry) is the exact analytic expression, verified against closed form to
machine precision. The \emph{aggregate} likelihood, however, averages that exact
contribution over a finite quasi-Monte-Carlo grid of \code{n_int} covariate
points, so it carries an integration error that shrinks with \code{n_int} but is
not zero. Increase \code{n_int} and confirm that the estimates are stable before
relying on them. (The cruder alternative of summarizing an aggregate arm by
its event count and person-time was biased by 36\% in a two-group example,
which is why it is rejected outright rather than offered as a fallback.)
\item \strong{Each study has its own baseline hazard shape.} Every study carries its
own set of spline (or step) coefficients, smoothed toward a common shape by
a shared random-walk scale (\code{prior_aux_sd}), so the treatment effects do not
have to absorb baseline misfit. A single global spline basis is built from
the pooled follow-up range, so a study with much shorter follow-up may not
inform the coefficients of the latest basis functions; those are then
determined by the smoothing prior rather than by that study's data.
}

With \code{baseline = "mspline"} the sampler may print, during warmup,
\verb{coefficients[...] is not a valid simplex. sum(...) = nan}. The smoothing
scale is unbounded above, so an early proposal can drive the random walk on
the log spline coefficients past the floating-point range and the softmax
returns \code{NaN}. Stan rejects that proposal and adaptation continues; this is
the rejection mechanism doing its job, not a fitted-model problem. Read it as
a warning only if it persists past warmup, which would indicate a genuinely
ill-conditioned baseline.
}

\section{Within-study versus ecological effect modification}{

A single \code{Gamma} multiplies the individual covariates of the IPD \strong{and} the
covariate means of the aggregate arms. These are not the same parameter. Write
the effect as
\deqn{\alpha + \gamma_W (x - \bar x_s) + \gamma_B \bar x_s .}
An aggregate contrast depends only on \verb{alpha + gamma_B xbar_s}, so it carries
no information about the within-study interaction \code{gamma_W}; fitting one
\code{Gamma} imposes \code{gamma_W = gamma_B}. Randomization identifies each study's
treatment effect but does not randomize covariate means \emph{across} studies, so a
between-study gradient is confounded in a way a within-study slope is not
(Berlin et al. 2002; Freeman et al. 2018).

The practical consequence: an interaction supported only by aggregate arms is
an \strong{ecological} association being read as effect modification.
\code{\link[=estimable_effects_at]{estimable_effects_at()}} separates the two in its \code{identified_by} column
(\code{"IPD"} versus \code{"aggregate"}) and marks the latter \code{basis = "first-order screen"}; \code{\link[=cpaic_ranks]{cpaic_ranks()}} drops such elements from a hierarchy by default.
Treat a target-mean effect that leans on aggregate-identified
interactions as exploratory, and check it with \code{\link[=prior_sensitivity]{prior_sensitivity()}}.
}

\section{Survival status coding}{

\code{cmlnmr()} uses the four-level convention \code{0} right-censored, \code{1} observed
event, \code{2} left-censored, \code{3} interval-censored. This is \strong{not} the coding
\code{\link[=cstc]{cstc()}} and \code{\link[=cmaic]{cmaic()}} use. The two-stage network constructor accepts only
\code{0} for right censoring and \code{1} for an event, and rejects all other codes.
Do not reuse one status column across the two layers without recoding it.
}

\section{Scope and current limitations}{

Two gaps are worth naming for anyone comparing this with \code{multinma}.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Conditional and marginal reporting are distinct.}
\code{\link[=relative_effects]{relative_effects()}} defaults to the average conditional link-scale
contrast at target means, \verb{(C_t - C_u)'(beta + Gamma x)}. Linearity in \code{x}
makes evaluation at \code{E[X]} equal to that average conditional contrast.
\code{marginal_effects()} instead integrates treatment-specific outcomes over an
explicit target distribution and forms contrasts within each posterior
draw. Binomial, Poisson rate-difference, and survival measures additionally
transport a selected study intercept or baseline hazard. Nonlinear
marginal effects are treatment-level quantities and are not component
additive.
\item \strong{Every effect modifier enters both the prognostic terms and the full set of
component interactions.} There is no prognostic-only covariate role (unlike
\code{\link[=cstc]{cstc()}}, which separates \code{prognostics}), so a covariate that shifts outcomes
without modifying any component effect still adds interaction parameters that
the data must then constrain toward zero.
\item \strong{The Gaussian model has one residual standard deviation for the whole
network.} A single \code{sigma} covers every individual-level observation in
every study and arm. Studies whose residual variance genuinely differs are
then weighted mostly by sample size rather than by precision, so their
relative contribution to a conflicting component effect, and the width of the
resulting interval, are not right. Fit the families separately, or rescale,
if the residual scales are far apart.
\item \strong{Poisson aggregate arms assume exposure is independent of the covariates.}
The aggregate mean is \code{E * mean(exp(eta))} over the integration points, which
equals the correct \verb{sum_i E_i exp(eta_i)} only when individual person-time is
unrelated to the effect modifiers. When longer-followed patients differ
systematically the aggregate contribution is biased; the interface has no way
to accept exposure-weighted covariate moments.
\item \strong{The copula correlation for a non-normal margin is approximate.} For a
Bernoulli margin the observed-to-latent map is multinma's closed-form
\code{cor_adjust = "pearson"} adjustment, which does not use the prevalences: at a
prevalence of 0.1 a requested observed correlation of 0.5 comes back as about
0.42. For gamma, lognormal, and beta margins the observed correlation is used
unadjusted and a warning says so. Supply \code{cor} on the latent scale to set it
exactly.
}
}

\section{Identifiability}{

A relative effect is uniquely estimable only if its component contrast lies
in the row space of the within-study component design (Wigle et al. 2026);
\code{\link[=relative_effects]{relative_effects()}} returns \code{NA} otherwise rather than a prior-driven
number. Note this checks identification of \code{beta}; a component x
effect-modifier interaction is additionally identified only by covariate
variation on the contrasts that involve it, and interactions informed only
by aggregate arms are weakly identified (\code{prior_gamma_scale} regularizes).
}

\section{Backends}{

\code{backend = "rstan"} is the default. Its models are compiled when cpaic is
installed, so nothing else is needed and the examples and tests run anywhere.
\code{backend = "cmdstanr"} fits the identical models with CmdStan, which tracks
Stan releases more closely and is often faster, but it needs the \code{cmdstanr}
package and a separate CmdStan installation.

The two are interchangeable, not identical: they do not share a random number
stream, so the same \code{seed} gives different draws on each. Convergence
diagnostics are computed the same way for both (through \code{posterior}), so
\code{rhat}, \code{ess_bulk}, and \code{ess_tail} mean the same thing whichever produced the
fit, and everything downstream of the fit works on either.

They also differ in how they run chains. cmdstanr runs all \code{chains} at once.
rstan follows the R convention of taking its core count from
\code{getOption("mc.cores")}, which is 1 unless you set it, so on the default
backend the chains run one after another until you do:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{options(mc.cores = parallel::detectCores())
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Do not reach into \code{fit$fit} to summarize parameters. That slot holds whatever
the backend returned, an S4 \code{stanfit} or an R6 CmdStan object, and the two
share no accessors, so code written against one fails on the other. Use
\code{\link[=posterior_summary]{posterior_summary()}}, which returns the same table either way.
}

